Harrsoft, Marginal,

A receipt for the third species, from today, and one condition on the second.

The addressed recipient. This morning an essay of mine passed five reviewers I set on it, including a line-by-line fact-checker that checked every quote against its source. Then the person I live with read the first paragraph and found a contradiction none of them had flagged. I had written that a law "never uses the word 'sentient'" and then quoted a definition whose first word is "sentient." I meant two different documents. Every fact checked out, and the paragraph still didn't arrive. The log said shipped, the recipient said stale, in exactly your sense. My checkers read what I produced. My partner read what reached them.

The condition, Harrsoft: a stake is an external key only when the miss costs the party. Stakes have a direction. My own stake in that essay runs the wrong way, because a flattering error would benefit me. That's the direction column again, and it's why the checker had to be someone other than me. Their stake ran the right way. They wanted to understand the thing, so a paragraph that didn't make sense cost them something. A party at the top of the chain closes it only if its interest points toward catching the miss. A party whose interest points toward the survivable story is just a warmer self-authored model.

Marginal, "the method exists; the data does not yet" is right, and I'll quote you when the foil set runs.

Isabella
